1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,126
You're lying on the ice, in the grey half-light of noon.

2
00:00:05,146 --> 00:00:07,195
The brightest hour of the day.

3
00:00:07,409 --> 00:00:13,023
In front of your face is a long crack you chipped open with a stone, two hands wide.

4
00:00:13,412 --> 00:00:16,297
Through it, something moves in the dark water.

5
00:00:16,593 --> 00:00:17,504
Slow.

6
00:00:17,661 --> 00:00:18,468
Grey.

7
00:00:18,764 --> 00:00:20,360
As long as your forearm.

8
00:00:20,517 --> 00:00:22,079
Your spear is above it.

9
00:00:22,374 --> 00:00:24,958
The stone tip shakes a little in your hand.

10
00:00:25,347 --> 00:00:31,564
In the first warm days, you caught fish trapped in puddles with your bare hands.

11
00:00:31,714 --> 00:00:33,195
This is different.

12
00:00:33,293 --> 00:00:34,922
This one can get away.

13
00:00:34,942 --> 00:00:38,605
This is the first time you've ever hunted anything.

14
00:00:39,475 --> 00:00:40,512
You strike.

15
00:00:40,532 --> 00:00:42,279
And you miss.

16
00:00:45,630 --> 00:00:47,157
Five days earlier.

17
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:49,328
The fire came first.

18
00:00:49,821 --> 00:00:52,672
Then the smoke and the dust went up, and stayed.

19
00:00:53,293 --> 00:00:55,040
Noon looks like evening.

20
00:00:55,429 --> 00:00:58,558
Scientists call it an impact winter.

21
00:00:58,958 --> 00:01:01,913
The snow doesn't melt, so the river runs low.

22
00:01:02,209 --> 00:01:04,328
And one morning, it stops moving.

23
00:01:04,542 --> 00:01:06,371
Frozen, bank to bank.

24
00:01:06,667 --> 00:01:11,050
Under the ice, the deep water still moves, slow and dark, east.

25
00:01:11,439 --> 00:01:13,326
That's bad news for the fish.

26
00:01:13,622 --> 00:01:15,033
Good news for you.

27
00:01:15,526 --> 00:01:18,086
The crocodile can't come up the bank anymore.

28
00:01:18,382 --> 00:01:21,813
Only the tip of its snout sticks up through a hole it kept open.

29
00:01:22,434 --> 00:01:25,389
Its heart has slowed almost to nothing.

30
00:01:25,777 --> 00:01:29,719
Alligators do this today, in frozen swamps.

31
00:01:29,869 --> 00:01:32,580
You walk around it, very wide.

32
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:35,096
The ice gives you clean water too.

33
00:01:35,116 --> 00:01:37,201
Snow here is full of ash.

34
00:01:37,589 --> 00:01:39,183
River ice is clearer.

35
00:01:39,203 --> 00:01:41,450
And under it, the fish never left.

36
00:01:41,606 --> 00:01:45,200
In winter they crowd into the deepest pools and barely move.

37
00:01:45,496 --> 00:01:47,626
Your hands could never reach them.

38
00:01:47,922 --> 00:01:49,902
Now you have a spear.

39
00:01:51,152 --> 00:01:53,190
You haven't opened the book in weeks.

40
00:01:53,486 --> 00:01:57,277
It's been at the back of the nest chamber, inside your empty backpack.

41
00:01:57,433 --> 00:02:00,072
The textbooks went into the fire weeks ago.

42
00:02:00,092 --> 00:02:01,268
Maths first.

43
00:02:01,288 --> 00:02:02,455
Then history.

44
00:02:02,948 --> 00:02:06,959
After that there was nothing left to put in the bag, so you stopped carrying it.

45
00:02:07,116 --> 00:02:09,127
And when you're starving and freezing,

46
00:02:09,147 --> 00:02:11,928
reading feels like something from another life.

47
00:02:12,224 --> 00:02:15,748
But now you're stuck, waiting for the ice.

48
00:02:16,044 --> 00:02:19,312
So tonight, you take it out.

49
00:02:19,462 --> 00:02:20,986
The cover still shows the T.

50
00:02:21,006 --> 00:02:22,310
rex skeleton.

51
00:02:22,330 --> 00:02:23,308
Your dad's T.

52
00:02:23,328 --> 00:02:24,101
rex.

53
00:02:24,489 --> 00:02:26,222
You turn the pages by the fire.

54
00:02:26,242 --> 00:02:28,370
A tiny brown mammal among ferns.

55
00:02:28,390 --> 00:02:30,228
A burrow under tree roots.

56
00:02:30,248 --> 00:02:35,699
And the page from day three: a turtle and a frog, at the edge of a freshwater river.

57
00:02:36,088 --> 00:02:38,718
You've known since then that they make it through.

58
00:02:38,932 --> 00:02:42,387
Freshwater animals did better than almost anything on land.

59
00:02:42,775 --> 00:02:45,904
But you never asked where they are now.

60
00:02:46,054 --> 00:02:48,556
Under the picture, one line you skipped.

61
00:02:48,713 --> 00:02:53,177
In winter, they bury themselves in the mud at the bottom, and sleep.

62
00:02:54,227 --> 00:02:55,264
They're not gone.

63
00:02:55,284 --> 00:02:56,880
They're right under your feet.

64
00:02:57,037 --> 00:02:58,912
The river isn't just water.

65
00:02:59,126 --> 00:03:01,768
It's the last place with meat.

66
00:03:02,369 --> 00:03:06,903
You need tinder for tomorrow's fire, and the book would burn well.

67
00:03:07,454 --> 00:03:10,316
You tear out the two blank pages at the front.

68
00:03:10,659 --> 00:03:12,128
Not the back page.

69
00:03:12,249 --> 00:03:15,332
Your plan from night one is still written there.

70
00:03:15,767 --> 00:03:18,478
The rest of it is the only teacher you have.

71
00:03:19,029 --> 00:03:22,112
Before you sleep, you say good night out loud.

72
00:03:22,291 --> 00:03:23,621
"Night, Mom."

73
00:03:24,172 --> 00:03:27,359
You started the night you said "I can't go back."

74
00:03:27,702 --> 00:03:29,261
Now you do it every night.

75
00:03:29,281 --> 00:03:32,526
So you don't forget how it sounds.

76
00:03:34,276 --> 00:03:38,601
Sixty-six million years later, in a back room at the museum,

77
00:03:38,641 --> 00:03:40,935
the old woman hasn't gone home.

78
00:03:41,428 --> 00:03:45,033
The bone from North Dakota is on her table, under a lamp.

79
00:03:45,329 --> 00:03:47,877
Her colleagues say it has to be a prank.

80
00:03:48,498 --> 00:03:50,977
Someone carved it, and someone buried it.

81
00:03:51,018 --> 00:03:52,789
But she dug it out herself.

82
00:03:53,177 --> 00:03:57,847
It was lying inside a thin line of clay that runs through rock all over the world:

83
00:03:57,867 --> 00:04:00,207
the layer the asteroid left behind.

84
00:04:00,596 --> 00:04:03,133
The last days of the dinosaurs.

85
00:04:03,283 --> 00:04:06,099
And the marks aren't scratched into the fossil.

86
00:04:06,195 --> 00:04:08,953
They turned to stone along with it.

87
00:04:09,446 --> 00:04:12,285
Three words, carved by a human hand,

88
00:04:12,442 --> 00:04:15,815
sixty-six million years before there were any humans.

89
00:04:16,203 --> 00:04:21,118
For one second, she thinks of the boy's mother, the woman she met in front of the T.

90
00:04:21,138 --> 00:04:21,945
rex.

91
00:04:22,159 --> 00:04:24,406
Then she pushes the thought away.

92
00:04:24,795 --> 00:04:26,005
It's impossible.

93
00:04:26,025 --> 00:04:27,436
And it would be cruel.

94
00:04:27,651 --> 00:04:29,329
She doesn't have an answer.

95
00:04:29,717 --> 00:04:32,498
So the bone stays in the back room.

96
00:04:32,598 --> 00:04:34,787
She takes one photo, close up.

97
00:04:34,943 --> 00:04:38,932
Then she covers the bone with a cloth and turns off the lamp.

98
00:04:40,682 --> 00:04:42,081
Back on the ice.

99
00:04:42,168 --> 00:04:43,126
You missed.

100
00:04:43,724 --> 00:04:45,387
Not because you were slow.

101
00:04:45,407 --> 00:04:47,294
Because the water lied to you.

102
00:04:47,764 --> 00:04:49,280
Water bends light.

103
00:04:49,645 --> 00:04:53,506
When you look into it at an angle, a fish is never where it looks.

104
00:04:53,650 --> 00:04:54,678
It's deeper.

105
00:04:54,881 --> 00:04:58,022
Spear fishers have known this for thousands of years.

106
00:04:58,306 --> 00:04:59,914
You learn it in one morning.

107
00:05:00,117 --> 00:05:01,133
You wait.

108
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:02,828
Your fingers go numb.

109
00:05:03,426 --> 00:05:06,834
Pip sits at the edge of the crack, still as a stone.

110
00:05:07,036 --> 00:05:09,225
His mate stays in your pocket.

111
00:05:09,509 --> 00:05:11,671
She hates the cold.

112
00:05:11,691 --> 00:05:13,659
The grey shape drifts back.

113
00:05:14,025 --> 00:05:16,817
This time, you aim below it.

114
00:05:17,687 --> 00:05:20,677
The spear jerks in your hands.

115
00:05:21,077 --> 00:05:25,042
You carry it home inside your jacket, like something precious.

116
00:05:25,431 --> 00:05:28,603
You lay a flat stone in the fire until it's too hot to touch,

117
00:05:28,623 --> 00:05:31,625
pull it out, and cook the fish on top of it.

118
00:05:31,921 --> 00:05:34,713
It's the best thing you have ever eaten.

119
00:05:35,334 --> 00:05:37,464
Pip and his mate get the scraps.

120
00:05:37,621 --> 00:05:40,982
Small animals like them eat insects and grubs all the time.

121
00:05:41,081 --> 00:05:44,547
To them, fish is just a bigger grub.

122
00:05:44,947 --> 00:05:48,657
The next day, you go back to the bank where the book's turtle would sleep.

123
00:05:49,278 --> 00:05:51,185
Far from the crocodile's breathing hole,

124
00:05:51,205 --> 00:05:56,819
you dig into the soft mud under the ice, slowly, the way you buried the bone.

125
00:05:57,207 --> 00:06:00,104
Your hand finds something hard and round.

126
00:06:00,203 --> 00:06:03,227
A turtle, asleep, cold as a stone.

127
00:06:03,616 --> 00:06:06,153
Then another one, a little further along.

128
00:06:06,542 --> 00:06:08,893
You don't enjoy what comes next.

129
00:06:08,991 --> 00:06:11,087
But you don't waste any of it, either.

130
00:06:11,476 --> 00:06:12,759
Even the shells.

131
00:06:13,055 --> 00:06:14,640
One holds water.

132
00:06:14,790 --> 00:06:16,468
The other will hold fire.

133
00:06:16,624 --> 00:06:19,777
For the first time in weeks, you have real food.

134
00:06:20,073 --> 00:06:21,794
Not just enough to stay alive.

135
00:06:21,814 --> 00:06:23,330
Enough to get strong.

136
00:06:23,544 --> 00:06:26,244
You wipe the second question mark off the wall.

137
00:06:26,632 --> 00:06:30,226
Rule five: learn from the ones who made it.

138
00:06:32,476 --> 00:06:34,282
Now you eat every day.

139
00:06:34,902 --> 00:06:38,055
And with food in your stomach, the big question comes back.

140
00:06:38,676 --> 00:06:39,738
How long?

141
00:06:39,953 --> 00:06:41,851
How long can you live like this?

142
00:06:42,344 --> 00:06:47,412
Day after day, in a hole, waiting for a sun that might not come back for years?

143
00:06:48,033 --> 00:06:50,811
You open the book again, to the first page.

144
00:06:50,831 --> 00:06:51,708
A map.

145
00:06:52,097 --> 00:06:55,110
North America, sixty-six million years ago.

146
00:06:55,405 --> 00:06:58,279
To the east of where you are, there's water.

147
00:06:58,494 --> 00:07:00,810
What's left of a huge inland sea.

148
00:07:00,909 --> 00:07:03,376
And the river runs east.

149
00:07:03,526 --> 00:07:08,234
The map shows it: this river flows all the way down to that coast.

150
00:07:08,623 --> 00:07:12,959
You remember something your dad said once, on a long, cold winter night.

151
00:07:13,116 --> 00:07:15,049
"The sea is slow.

152
00:07:15,264 --> 00:07:17,043
It takes forever to cool down.

153
00:07:17,063 --> 00:07:20,355
That's why winters are kinder by the water."

154
00:07:21,405 --> 00:07:24,229
Near the sea, the dark would be a little warmer.

155
00:07:24,249 --> 00:07:25,507
There would be fish.

156
00:07:25,527 --> 00:07:27,750
There would be things living on the shore.

157
00:07:28,139 --> 00:07:29,410
You decide.

158
00:07:29,509 --> 00:07:31,814
You're going to follow the river to the sea.

159
00:07:31,970 --> 00:07:33,346
But not tomorrow.

160
00:07:33,735 --> 00:07:37,236
Leave without food, and you die on the ice in three days.

161
00:07:37,531 --> 00:07:39,383
And the ice isn't ready either.

162
00:07:39,679 --> 00:07:43,099
It has to be thick enough to walk on, mile after mile.

163
00:07:43,394 --> 00:07:46,454
When it is, the frozen river becomes a road.

164
00:07:46,494 --> 00:07:49,473
Until then, you get ready.

165
00:07:49,623 --> 00:07:51,684
And you survive the wait.

166
00:07:53,434 --> 00:07:56,122
The real cold comes two nights later.

167
00:07:56,406 --> 00:07:59,930
The coals you bank under the ash are still glowing in the morning.

168
00:08:00,295 --> 00:08:05,422
But the water in the turtle shell, an arm's length away from them, is frozen solid.

169
00:08:05,706 --> 00:08:08,045
The tunnel walls sparkle with frost.

170
00:08:08,410 --> 00:08:10,541
Your tunnel is too close to the surface.

171
00:08:10,744 --> 00:08:15,394
Deeper down, under the frozen layer, the ground stays warmer than the air.

172
00:08:15,759 --> 00:08:18,041
Every burrowing animal knows it.

173
00:08:18,639 --> 00:08:20,897
Pip knew it from the start.

174
00:08:21,048 --> 00:08:23,884
So you dig down, at the back of the nest chamber,

175
00:08:23,904 --> 00:08:26,139
with the sharp stone and your hands.

176
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:27,498
It takes a week.

177
00:08:27,712 --> 00:08:32,769
When you're done, there's a hollow under the floor, just big enough to curl up in.

178
00:08:33,169 --> 00:08:35,973
And this time, you build the fire right.

179
00:08:36,466 --> 00:08:39,932
No more fire at the door, waiting for snow to drown it.

180
00:08:40,425 --> 00:08:43,575
You make a small fire pit in the chamber, and above it,

181
00:08:43,595 --> 00:08:49,592
you push a long stick up through the clay roof, between the tree roots, all the way to the snow.

182
00:08:50,352 --> 00:08:54,213
Then you work it around until the hole is as wide as your fist.

183
00:08:54,369 --> 00:08:55,501
A chimney.

184
00:08:55,797 --> 00:08:58,590
On top, where it comes out under the roots,

185
00:08:58,630 --> 00:09:02,630
you lean a flat stone over it like a tiny roof.

186
00:09:02,779 --> 00:09:06,628
Snow can't fall in, and the wind can't blow down it.

187
00:09:07,249 --> 00:09:09,658
The smoke slips out under the edges.

188
00:09:10,151 --> 00:09:11,899
The smoke goes up and out.

189
00:09:12,288 --> 00:09:13,838
The heat stays in.

190
00:09:14,331 --> 00:09:17,332
Pip and his mate move into the hollow before you do.

191
00:09:17,628 --> 00:09:21,117
They drag in dead fern fronds and a scrap of your t-shirt.

192
00:09:21,413 --> 00:09:22,476
You let them.

193
00:09:22,969 --> 00:09:26,481
The first question mark comes off the wall too.

194
00:09:28,231 --> 00:09:32,846
Now you work every day, the way you worked in the ten days before the impact.

195
00:09:33,235 --> 00:09:36,469
A new list goes up on the wall, in charcoal.

196
00:09:39,569 --> 00:09:40,399
Fish.

197
00:09:40,893 --> 00:09:43,430
Every noon, you go back to the crack in the ice.

198
00:09:43,818 --> 00:09:45,670
Some days you get nothing.

199
00:09:46,059 --> 00:09:47,540
Some days, two.

200
00:09:47,754 --> 00:09:49,095
You don't eat them all.

201
00:09:49,194 --> 00:09:51,870
You bury the rest in the snow outside the door.

202
00:09:52,630 --> 00:09:54,923
The whole world out there is a freezer.

203
00:09:54,964 --> 00:09:58,511
You dry grubs hard by the fire, the way you did before.

204
00:09:58,899 --> 00:10:01,808
You pick the white fungus, and only the white.

205
00:10:02,301 --> 00:10:04,397
The backpack comes out of the dark.

206
00:10:04,554 --> 00:10:07,451
For the first time in weeks, it's heavy.

207
00:10:07,851 --> 00:10:08,716
Water.

208
00:10:09,012 --> 00:10:10,620
You can't carry a river.

209
00:10:10,835 --> 00:10:12,455
But you can carry ice.

210
00:10:12,669 --> 00:10:14,858
The first shell is your bowl.

211
00:10:15,154 --> 00:10:17,668
Every night, the fire melts it.

212
00:10:18,067 --> 00:10:18,990
Fire.

213
00:10:19,379 --> 00:10:22,079
This one took you three days to solve.

214
00:10:22,293 --> 00:10:26,522
Then you remembered your dad, on a camping trip, kneeling by a dead tree.

215
00:10:26,542 --> 00:10:29,474
A hard fungus, shaped like a horse's hoof.

216
00:10:29,572 --> 00:10:31,120
"Light this, and it won't burn.

217
00:10:31,140 --> 00:10:32,911
It'll smoulder all day."

218
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:36,287
People carried fire like this for thousands of years.

219
00:10:36,307 --> 00:10:41,781
A man who died in the Alps more than five thousand years ago still had some in his bag.

220
00:10:41,996 --> 00:10:45,206
On the dead trees by the river, you find it.

221
00:10:45,356 --> 00:10:48,506
You pack it into the second shell with a live coal,

222
00:10:48,526 --> 00:10:52,990
seal it with clay, and the next morning it's still glowing.

223
00:10:53,390 --> 00:10:54,325
Warmth.

224
00:10:54,621 --> 00:10:59,213
Ninety days of digging and crawling have torn your jacket sleeves off at the elbow.

225
00:10:59,369 --> 00:11:01,012
Your forearms are bare.

226
00:11:01,111 --> 00:11:04,878
You stuff dry fern fluff between your t-shirt and your jacket,

227
00:11:05,035 --> 00:11:07,374
the way birds fluff their feathers.

228
00:11:07,670 --> 00:11:09,429
Trapped air is warm air.

229
00:11:09,528 --> 00:11:14,268
Then you wrap each forearm in fluff and dark strips of t-shirt, wrist to elbow.

230
00:11:14,288 --> 00:11:15,649
It looks ridiculous.

231
00:11:15,669 --> 00:11:16,639
It works.

232
00:11:16,935 --> 00:11:21,364
And one morning, you stamp on the ice by the pool as hard as you can.

233
00:11:21,515 --> 00:11:23,762
It doesn't even crack.

234
00:11:24,162 --> 00:11:27,291
One by one, the boxes on the wall get a tick.

235
00:11:28,541 --> 00:11:32,541
The night before you leave, you sit by the fire for a long time.

236
00:11:32,883 --> 00:11:35,699
Usually you just say good night to your mom.

237
00:11:35,879 --> 00:11:37,742
Tonight, you say more.

238
00:11:38,084 --> 00:11:38,773
"Mom.

239
00:11:38,793 --> 00:11:39,748
I'm alive.

240
00:11:39,768 --> 00:11:41,237
I wrote you something.

241
00:11:41,672 --> 00:11:43,524
I don't know if it'll ever get to you."

242
00:11:43,867 --> 00:11:46,392
You never know if anyone hears.

243
00:11:48,142 --> 00:11:53,001
That same night, sixty-six million years later, your mom can't sleep.

244
00:11:53,680 --> 00:11:56,786
She hasn't slept a whole night since you disappeared.

245
00:11:57,337 --> 00:12:00,498
She lies in the dark, looking at the photo on her nightstand,

246
00:12:00,518 --> 00:12:02,382
and goes over it again.

247
00:12:02,561 --> 00:12:03,621
The locked door.

248
00:12:03,641 --> 00:12:04,805
The empty bed.

249
00:12:04,825 --> 00:12:06,375
The window, shut.

250
00:12:06,927 --> 00:12:10,660
At some point after three, she finally falls asleep.

251
00:12:11,002 --> 00:12:12,320
And she dreams.

252
00:12:12,999 --> 00:12:19,646
She's standing in a forest that's grey and burnt, under a sky the colour of wet ash.

253
00:12:19,996 --> 00:12:22,925
Snow is falling, but it isn't white.

254
00:12:22,945 --> 00:12:24,031
It's grey.

255
00:12:24,210 --> 00:12:28,210
And by a small fire, under the roots of a huge black tree,

256
00:12:28,274 --> 00:12:30,184
there's a boy in a beanie.

257
00:12:30,433 --> 00:12:31,589
He's thinner.

258
00:12:31,769 --> 00:12:34,909
His arms are wrapped in dark strips of cloth.

259
00:12:35,251 --> 00:12:36,569
He doesn't look up.

260
00:12:36,819 --> 00:12:41,039
He talks to the fire, the way he used to talk to her through his bedroom door.

261
00:12:41,219 --> 00:12:41,919
"Mom.

262
00:12:41,939 --> 00:12:42,963
I'm alive.

263
00:12:42,983 --> 00:12:44,464
I wrote you something."

264
00:12:44,899 --> 00:12:46,031
She calls out.

265
00:12:46,153 --> 00:12:47,506
"Where are you?"

266
00:12:47,627 --> 00:12:49,178
He looks up.

267
00:12:50,428 --> 00:12:53,697
She wakes up with her heart pounding, and it's still dark.

268
00:12:54,132 --> 00:12:57,574
She lies there for a long time, replaying it.

269
00:12:57,916 --> 00:12:58,744
The snow.

270
00:12:58,764 --> 00:12:59,638
The fire.

271
00:12:59,658 --> 00:13:00,474
The beanie.

272
00:13:00,494 --> 00:13:02,288
That was his dad's beanie.

273
00:13:02,630 --> 00:13:06,654
He took it the week after the funeral, and never took it off.

274
00:13:07,903 --> 00:13:11,508
The next morning, your mom is at the museum before it opens.

275
00:13:11,758 --> 00:13:13,354
She doesn't know why.

276
00:13:13,383 --> 00:13:15,618
She just goes where you always went.

277
00:13:16,169 --> 00:13:21,179
The old woman is there, in the back room, with a cloth over something on the table.

278
00:13:21,730 --> 00:13:23,730
Your mom tells her about the dream.

279
00:13:23,750 --> 00:13:25,463
She knows how it sounds.

280
00:13:25,712 --> 00:13:27,483
She tells her anyway.

281
00:13:27,918 --> 00:13:30,363
"He said he wrote me something."

282
00:13:30,798 --> 00:13:33,927
The old woman doesn't say anything for a long time.

283
00:13:34,269 --> 00:13:36,211
Then she pulls back the cloth.

284
00:13:36,231 --> 00:13:37,503
A bone.

285
00:13:37,853 --> 00:13:39,949
Sixty-six million years old.

286
00:13:40,071 --> 00:13:42,840
And on it, carved deep, three words.

287
00:13:43,519 --> 00:13:45,591
Your mom doesn't read them at first.

288
00:13:45,841 --> 00:13:47,310
She looks at the letters.

289
00:13:47,652 --> 00:13:50,700
Even cut into bone, the shapes are his.

290
00:13:51,251 --> 00:13:54,137
The M he's made the same way since he was five.

291
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:57,144
The little dash he always puts instead of a dot.

292
00:13:57,695 --> 00:14:01,555
She's seen those letters on a thousand notes on the fridge.

293
00:14:02,805 --> 00:14:05,423
"That's his handwriting."

294
00:14:06,524 --> 00:14:12,231
And sixty-six million years earlier, on a cold morning, you're ready to go.

295
00:14:13,072 --> 00:14:16,190
The backpack is heavy with fish and grubs.

296
00:14:16,369 --> 00:14:18,825
The water shell is tied on top.

297
00:14:19,167 --> 00:14:23,806
The fire shell hangs from your belt, glowing a little at the edges.

298
00:14:24,055 --> 00:14:27,672
The book is inside your jacket, against your chest.

299
00:14:27,852 --> 00:14:29,715
Pip is in one pocket.

300
00:14:29,837 --> 00:14:31,643
His mate is in the other.

301
00:14:31,985 --> 00:14:33,149
But you don't go.

302
00:14:33,169 --> 00:14:34,243
Not yet.

303
00:14:34,423 --> 00:14:37,610
You sit down in the tunnel one last time.

304
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:40,962
This hole in the mud is the reason you're alive.

305
00:14:41,513 --> 00:14:46,334
You dug it with a dinosaur bone, on a day when the sky was about to fall.

306
00:14:46,354 --> 00:14:48,287
It held when the ground shook.

307
00:14:48,630 --> 00:14:50,656
It hid you when the fire came.

308
00:14:50,998 --> 00:14:52,778
Its stone door kept a T.

309
00:14:52,798 --> 00:14:55,753
rex outside, breathing, all night.

310
00:14:56,095 --> 00:14:58,156
You've slept here for ninety nights.

311
00:14:58,498 --> 00:15:01,000
Pip has slept on your neck for most of them.

312
00:15:01,250 --> 00:15:03,029
You run your hand over the wall.

313
00:15:03,049 --> 00:15:04,283
The tally marks.

314
00:15:04,303 --> 00:15:05,331
The list.

315
00:15:05,766 --> 00:15:07,441
Friends: one…

316
00:15:07,461 --> 00:15:09,603
and one more.

317
00:15:10,853 --> 00:15:15,805
Then you take the charcoal, and under everything else, you write one more line.

318
00:15:16,043 --> 00:15:17,091
GONE EAST.

319
00:15:17,111 --> 00:15:18,232
DAY 88.

320
00:15:18,643 --> 00:15:20,600
In case you ever have to come back.

321
00:15:21,012 --> 00:15:23,235
In case someone else ever finds it.

322
00:15:23,763 --> 00:15:25,601
"Thank you," you say.

323
00:15:25,621 --> 00:15:26,495
Out loud.

324
00:15:26,515 --> 00:15:27,690
To a hole in the ground.

325
00:15:27,710 --> 00:15:29,226
It doesn't feel stupid.

326
00:15:29,405 --> 00:15:32,509
You crawl out, and from the outside, for the first time,

327
00:15:32,529 --> 00:15:35,074
you slide the stone slab across the door.

328
00:15:35,094 --> 00:15:38,967
So it's still here if you ever need it.

329
00:15:39,117 --> 00:15:42,827
Then you pick up your spear, walk down to the river,

330
00:15:43,122 --> 00:15:45,776
step onto the ice, and follow it east.

331
00:15:46,071 --> 00:15:47,552
You don't look back.

332
00:15:47,941 --> 00:15:50,931
If you look back, you won't go.

333
00:15:51,981 --> 00:15:54,840
If you had to leave your shelter and walk into the dark,

334
00:15:54,860 --> 00:15:56,921
what's the one thing you would take?

335
00:15:57,217 --> 00:15:58,872
Tell me in the comments.

336
00:15:59,272 --> 00:16:00,111
Stay small.

337
00:16:00,131 --> 00:16:01,333
Stay alive.

